*Application for Assistant Professor (Research and Education) in Mechanical Engineering, Advanced Propulsion and Vehicle Systems, post 58193*

I am writing to apply for the *Assistant Professor (Research and Education) in Mechanical Engineering* post (*58193*, Grade 8) in the *Department of Mechanical Engineering*, *School of Engineering*, at the University of Birmingham. I am currently a Research Assistant at the *Power Electronics and Machines Centre (PEMC), University of Nottingham*, having completed my *PhD in Electrical Engineering at Southeast University (SEU), Nanjing*, in September 2025 with the dissertation _Multi-physics Analysis and Design of Hairpin Winding PM Traction Machines in Electric Vehicles_. My doctoral and postdoctoral work is well-aligned with the research areas named in the post, in particular *integrated electrified and hybrid powertrains*, *powertrain decarbonisation strategies*, and the *AI-driven* component of *intelligent, connected and automated vehicle systems*, and I would be glad to contribute to the Department's research and teaching on advanced propulsion and vehicle systems and the UK's net-zero transport agenda.

My primary research focus is the *multi-physics design and thermal management of high power-density EV traction machines* for next-generation electrified powertrains. My _IEEE Trans. Transportation Electrification_ paper _High-Fidelity Lumped Parameter Thermal Model for Oil-Cooling Hairpin Winding PM Machines in EVs_ develops a high-resolution lumped-parameter network coupled to a fluid-solid oil-cooling model, validated against AVL dynamometer measurements. The same programme covers *_n_-layer flat-wire and hairpin winding design* for high power-density EV PM machines, *multi-objective optimisation* coupling electromagnetic, thermal and mechanical objectives, and *PWM-loss interaction analysis* that links converter behaviour to thermal-degradation limits. This is directly relevant to the post's interest in *integrated electrified and hybrid powertrains*, *thermal (HVAC) systems*, and *efficiency, degradation and lifecycle performance*.

I have also gained substantive *industrial development experience* through a multi-year collaboration with *Inovance*, a world-leading EV traction-machine supplier, on two oil-cooled PM traction machines, an *18 000 rpm 113 kW A0-class machine* (138 Nm, 7.78 kW/kg, 95.3% peak efficiency) and a *20 000 rpm 270 kW A-class machine* (380 Nm, 7.87 kW/kg, 97.4% peak efficiency). Each was taken through the full industrial cycle of multi-physics design, engineering-drawing release and tolerance specification, and AVL dynamometer characterisation across the full efficiency map. This combined academic and industrial background, together with my current PEMC role on the *Sustainable Multi-Sector Electrification Using Advanced Integrated Motor Drive Technologies (AIMD)* programme (Nottingham, Bristol and Newcastle), which covers both next-generation magnet-free synchronous machines and integrated motor-drive power electronics, should support competitive applications to *EPSRC*, *Innovate UK*, the *Advanced Propulsion Centre (APC)*, the *Faraday Institution* and the *Aerospace Technology Institute (ATI)*, as well as direct industrial co-funding from EV and powertrain partners.

A second research focus develops *AI-driven surrogate modelling and physics-grounded digital twins* for electric propulsion system design, addressing the post's interest in *intelligent, connected and automated vehicle systems empowered by AI*. My ICEM 2026 paper _A Deep Learning Based Magnetic Field Surrogate Modeling Method for Interior Permanent Magnet Machine in EVs_ introduces an implicit physics-knowledge embedding approach in which a deep neural network learns to map machine design parameters to the *magnetic vector potential field*, reaching *R#super[2] = 0.9996* for average torque and *0.9730* for iron loss while reducing inference time by *more than 94%* against direct finite-element analysis. The surrogate sits on an *in-house Python finite-element code package* I have developed (electromagnetic, structural-modal, mechanical-stress) and provides the substrate for *digital-twin* studies of propulsion components, fast design-space exploration, and AI-assisted control development. My methodological foundation in *multi-physics modelling of energy-conversion systems* extends naturally toward *hydrogen integration*, *fuel-flexible systems* and *whole-system efficiency optimisation*, and I would be glad to develop in those directions through interdisciplinary collaboration within the Department and with Birmingham's wider net-zero transport community.

My research record includes *22 journal papers, of which 17 are in IEEE Transactions* (across _IEEE Trans. Industrial Electronics_, _Transportation Electrification_, _Industry Applications_, and _Energy Conversion_), 18 peer-reviewed conference papers at IEEE IEMDC, IEEE ECCE, ICEM and ICEMS, and 9 patents. The work has been recognised by the *2025 IEEE TEC Electric Vehicle Design Competition (1#super[st] Prize)*, the *2025 IEEE Trans. Transportation Electrification Prize Paper Award (2#super[nd] Prize)*, the *1#super[st] Prize in the IEEE IAS CMD Thesis Contest (non-PhD category)*, the *CIEEC Best Paper Award (2023)*, and the *Jiangsu Province Outstanding Master Degree Thesis Award (2022)*. I also serve as *Co-Organiser and Co-Chair of an ICEM 2026 Special Session* on _Next-Generation Electrified Transport_ in Funchal, Madeira, Portugal, and as peer reviewer for _IEEE Trans. Industry Applications_, _Energy Conversion_, and _Transportation Electrification_.

I would be glad to contribute to teaching across the Department's portfolio at undergraduate, MSc, CPD and short-course level, in areas such as *electric machines and drives, electrified powertrain design, multi-physics and thermal-management modelling, finite-element principles and FEA-based design, and AI for engineering*, drawing on the in-house Python finite-element code and the deep-learning surrogate as supporting material. During my doctoral studies I served as teaching assistant on the SEU course _Integrated Practice of Electrical Machine Systems_, preparing tutorial material, leading Q&A, reviewing project proposals, and contributing mark recommendations. I have also supported junior PhD and MSc colleagues on machine modelling, thermal-network construction and PWM-loss coupling.

My CV is attached, along with a detailed response to the selection criteria. I would be glad to discuss my research and teaching plans further at interview.

Yours sincerely,

